export const corpus: CorpusDocument[] = [
  { workspace: "brindille", slug: "hygiene-rules", title: "Hygiene rules", body: "# Hygiene rules\n\nBrindille follows the French food hygiene regulation, and the inspector can come at any hour. The rules below are the ones that matter every day; the full plan is in the binder behind the counter.\n\n## People\n\nHands are washed on arrival, after the toilet, after handling money, after touching the bin and every time you switch from raw to baked. Hair is covered in the bakehouse. Jewellery stays at home except a plain ring. Anyone with a stomach illness stays at home for 48 hours after the last symptom and tells the manager.\n\n## Temperatures\n\nFridges are checked and written down twice a day, at opening and at 15:00. Hot products are kept above 63 degrees or sold within two hours of baking. Cream products are made the same day and thrown away at closing, no exception.\n\n## Cleaning\n\nThe bakehouse floor is washed every evening and the tables after every batch. The display is emptied and wiped every evening. The deep clean of the oven and the extraction is on Sunday evening, logged in the binder with the name of who did it.\n\n## Waste\n\nUnsold bread goes in the collection bags for the charity at 19:30. Everything else goes in the bins, sorted. Nothing is ever taken home and sold.\n" },
  { workspace: "brindille", slug: "opening-checklist", title: "Opening checklist", body: "# Opening checklist\n\nEvery Brindille bakery opens at 07:00, and the first customer at 07:01 should find bread on the shelf, a clean floor and a smiling person at the till. This checklist is what the opening baker and the opening sales person do, in order, from 04:30.\n\n## The baker, from 04:30\n\nSwitch on the oven first, before anything else, so it reaches 240 degrees by 05:30. Check the fridge temperatures and write them on the sheet: the walk-in at 3 degrees or below, the display fridge at 4 or below. A fridge above 5 degrees means the food inside is checked piece by piece and the manager is called before 06:00. Take the dough out of the retarder in the order of the plan: baguettes first, then the loaves, then the viennoiseries. First bake in the oven at 05:45, second at 06:30, third at 07:15 so the shelf is full when the school rush starts at 08:00.\n\n## The sales person, from 06:15\n\nWipe the counter and the display, put out the price tags, check the till float of 150 euros, and switch on the card terminal. Put the pastries in the display from the left, the bread on the shelves behind, the sandwiches in the cold display with the dates on them. Unlock the door at 06:58 and switch the sign.\n\n## Both\n\nA quick look at the sales board for the day: what was left yesterday, what is ordered for today, which special is on. If a delivery of flour or butter comes in the morning, the baker signs for it and checks the temperature of the butter at the door; butter above 8 degrees goes back on the truck.\n\n## Allergens\n\nEvery product on the shelf has its allergen card, and the card is checked on Monday against the recipe book. A customer who asks about an ingredient gets the card, not a guess.\n" },
  { workspace: "brindille", slug: "staffing-rota", title: "Staffing rota rules", body: "# Staffing rota rules\n\nA bakery runs on its rota. The rota is written two weeks ahead by the manager, posted on the wall and in the app on the Thursday, and changed after that only by swaps agreed by both people and the manager.\n\n## Shifts\n\nOpening baker 04:30 to 12:00. Second baker 06:00 to 13:30. Opening sales 06:15 to 14:00. Afternoon sales 13:30 to 19:45. Closing baker, on Fridays and Saturdays only, 12:00 to 19:30. Everyone gets a 30-minute break, paid, after four hours.\n\n## Days\n\nNobody works more than six days in a row or more than two Sundays a month. A full-time week is 35 hours; hours above 35 in a week are overtime, paid at the legal rate, and need the manager's agreement before, not after. Public holidays are open except 1 May and 25 December, and a public holiday worked is paid double.\n\n## Swaps and absences\n\nA swap is written in the app by the person giving the shift and accepted by the person taking it; the manager approves within a day. Sickness is reported to the manager by phone before 05:00 for a morning shift and before 11:00 for an afternoon shift. Being late three times in a month means a conversation with the manager.\n\n## Students and seasonal staff\n\nStudent contracts are for Saturdays and the school holidays, at most 20 hours a week during term. Summer and Christmas seasonal staff are hired for six weeks and trained on the opening checklist on their first day.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "backups-and-restore", title: "Database backups and restore", body: "# Database backups and restore\n\nThere is one production database for the platform, and losing it would mean losing bookings, invoices and the badge events that bill the customers. Backups are not a backup until a restore has been tested, so this page covers both, and the restore test is on the calendar.\n\n## What is backed up\n\nThe production database: a full snapshot every night at 02:00 Paris time, kept for 35 days, and the write-ahead log shipped continuously, so the database can be restored to any second in the last 35 days. Monthly snapshots are kept for 13 months, the same as the badge events, in the second French region. The telemetry store is backed up weekly only; it can be rebuilt from the hubs, which keep 30 days of readings.\n\n## Where\n\nThe nightly snapshots are in the primary region; a copy goes to the second region within an hour. Both are encrypted with a key the database provider does not hold. Nothing leaves the EU.\n\n## Restore to a point in time\n\nOpen the database console, choose the production instance, choose \"restore to a point in time\" and the second you want, and restore into a new instance named with the date. Never restore over the running production instance. The new instance takes between twenty and forty minutes to be ready. Then run the verification script from the operations repository against it: it checks the row counts of the twelve largest tables against the nightly report and the last badge event's timestamp.\n\n## Switching over\n\nIf the restored instance is the one to keep, the switch is a change of the database address in the secrets manager followed by a rolling restart of the three services, in this order: fleet service, booking API, web app. Hubs reconnect on their own. The old instance is kept, stopped, for seven days.\n\n## The restore test\n\nThe first Wednesday of every month, the on-call secondary restores last night's snapshot into a fresh instance, runs the verification script, writes the result and the time it took in the operations channel, and deletes the instance. A test that takes more than an hour, or that fails, is a Sev 3 incident with a review. The last twelve results are in the operations wiki.\n\n## Exports for customers\n\nA customer can ask for an export of their data. Exports come from a restored copy, never from production, and are produced by the data team as CSV files per table, encrypted, with a password sent by another channel. The export job is logged with who asked, who ran it and what it contained.\n\n## What is not backed up\n\nAnything on a laptop. Anything in a chat channel. Files in the shared drive are versioned by the provider for 30 days but not backed up by Oriel; a document that matters belongs in the wiki or the repository.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "data-retention-and-privacy", title: "Data retention and privacy", body: "# Data retention and privacy\n\nOriel is a data processor for its customers, the delivery companies whose couriers use the hubs, and a data controller for its own employees and for the couriers who sign up directly. This page says what is kept, for how long, and why. The legal basis for each category is in the register of processing kept by the privacy lead.\n\n## Courier badge data\n\nA badge event (badge id, hub id, slot, timestamp) is needed to bill the customer and to open the slot. It is kept in full for 13 months, which covers one year of invoices plus the dispute window, then aggregated to hourly counts per hub and deleted. A courier's name and phone number come from the customer, are used only to send the one-time badge code, and are deleted 30 days after the badge is deactivated.\n\n## Hub telemetry\n\nBattery voltage, temperature, charger state and door sensor readings are kept at full resolution for 90 days, at one-minute resolution for two years, and at hourly resolution forever, because the long series is how the firmware team finds slow failures. Telemetry has no personal data in it; the slot-to-badge link is in the badge events, not here.\n\n## Video\n\nHubs have a camera on the door for safety. Footage is kept for 7 days on the hub and never leaves it unless an incident is logged, in which case the clip for that incident is copied to the incident record and kept for one year. Footage is viewed only by the two people named in the incident procedure.\n\n## Customer accounts\n\nAccount, contract and invoice data are kept for the contract plus 10 years, as French accounting law requires. Support conversations are kept for three years. Marketing consent is recorded with the date and the wording shown, and is honoured within 48 hours of being withdrawn.\n\n## Employees\n\nThe personnel file is kept for five years after the person leaves; payroll records for ten. Applications from people who were not hired are deleted after two years unless they asked to be kept on file. Badge access logs for the offices are kept for three months.\n\n## Requests from people\n\nAnyone can ask what Oriel holds about them, ask for it to be corrected or deleted, or object to a use. Requests go to the privacy mailbox and are answered within one month, in writing, by the privacy lead. A request from a courier goes through the customer who employs them unless the courier signed up directly.\n\n## Where data lives\n\nEverything is hosted in the EU, with the database in France and backups in a second French region. No personal data is sent to a provider outside the EU. The list of providers, with what each one does, is published on the website and updated when it changes.\n\n## Deleting on time\n\nDeletion is a scheduled job per category, run nightly, with a report on the data channel each Monday saying what was deleted. A job that fails twice in a row pages the on-call engineer.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "deploying-the-platform", title: "Deploying the platform", body: "# Deploying the platform\n\nThe platform is three services (the booking API, the fleet service that talks to hubs, and the web app) and one database. Each is deployed on its own, from the main branch, by the pipeline. This page is how a change gets to production and how it comes back if it should not have.\n\n## The path of a change\n\nA change is a pull request with tests, reviewed by one person who did not write it, merged into main by the author once the checks are green. Merging deploys to staging within ten minutes. Production is a manual step in the pipeline, pressed by the author during deployment hours: Monday to Thursday, 10:00 to 16:00 Paris time. There are no production deployments on Fridays, on the day before a public holiday, or during a Sev 1 or 2 incident.\n\n## Staging\n\nStaging runs against a copy of production data from the night before, with the personal fields replaced by generated values. Two real hubs in the Lyon depot are connected to staging, so a fleet service change is tested against hardware before it meets the street. Staging is reset every night at 03:00.\n\n## Database changes\n\nA migration ships in its own pull request before the code that uses it, and must be backwards compatible with the running version: add a column, never rename one in the same release; make it nullable first, fill it, then add the constraint in a later migration. A migration that locks a big table runs at 05:00 through the maintenance job, not from the pipeline. Every migration has a tested down migration.\n\n## Rolling out\n\nThe booking API and the web app roll out one instance at a time behind the load balancer, with the health check holding traffic until the new instance answers. The fleet service is different: hubs keep a long connection to it, so a deployment moves hubs over in batches of 10 % with a five-minute wait between batches, and the pipeline stops if the hub reconnection rate drops.\n\n## Feature flags\n\nAnything a customer can see goes behind a flag, off by default, turned on per customer from the admin page. A flag that has been on for everyone for a month is removed, with its code, in the next release. Flags are not a substitute for tests.\n\n## Rolling back\n\nRolling back is a button in the pipeline that redeploys the previous version, and anyone on the on-call rotation can press it without asking. It takes about four minutes for the API and the web app, and about twenty for the fleet service because of the batches. A rolled-back change is not redeployed until the review has said why it failed.\n\n## Hub firmware\n\nFirmware is not deployed by this pipeline. A firmware release is signed by two people on the firmware team, pushed to the two depot hubs first, then to a canary group of 5 % of the fleet for a week, then to everyone in batches by city. A hub that fails to boot the new firmware falls back to the previous one on its own.\n\n## The checklist before pressing production\n\nThe staging deployment is green. The change has a flag if a customer can see it. The migration, if any, is already in production. It is a deployment hour. The on-call primary knows. You will be at your laptop for the next hour.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "expenses-and-travel", title: "Expenses and travel", body: "# Expenses and travel\n\nOriel pays back the money you spend for work. The rule of thumb: if you would be embarrassed to explain a receipt to a colleague, do not submit it. Claims go in the expenses tool with a photo of the receipt within 30 days of the spend; claims older than 60 days are refused.\n\n## What is covered\n\nTravel between the Paris and Lyon offices by train in second class, booked through the travel tool so the ticket is paid by the company directly. Taxis and ride-hailing only when public transport is not reasonable: before 06:30, after 22:30, with heavy equipment, or in a city where there is no transit line to the site. Meals on a day with travel are covered up to 25 euros for lunch and 35 euros for dinner, including drinks; a receipt is required above 10 euros. A hotel night is covered up to 140 euros in Paris and 110 euros elsewhere in France, breakfast included. Trips abroad are covered at the real cost, booked in advance through the travel tool.\n\n## What is not covered\n\nAlcohol outside a meal, fines, upgrades of any kind, personal insurance, and anything bought for a personal purpose. A meal shared with people from outside Oriel is a client meal and is approved by a department head before the event, not after.\n\n## Site visits\n\nMost travel at Oriel is to a hub site: a car park, a depot or a street where a charging hub is installed. For a site visit, the technician on duty books the trip, and the hub reference goes in the expense claim so the cost lands on the right project. Safety boots and a high-visibility jacket are provided by the company and are not an expense.\n\n## Company cards\n\nTeam leads and the field technicians hold a company card for purchases under 500 euros. Every card payment still needs a receipt in the tool within 30 days. A card payment with no receipt after 60 days is treated as a personal advance and deducted from the next payslip, after a reminder.\n\n## Equipment\n\nLaptops, screens, keyboards and phones are ordered by IT from the hardware catalogue, never bought on expenses. Small items for home work (a cable, a stand) are allowed up to 60 euros a year on expenses with a receipt.\n\n## Mileage\n\nUsing your own car for work is allowed only when there is no train and no company vehicle. Mileage is paid at the official French rate for your car's fiscal power, and the claim must say the start, the end and the reason for the trip. Parking at the destination is covered; parking at the office is not.\n\n## Approval\n\nClaims under 100 euros are approved automatically unless the tool flags them. Claims between 100 and 1,000 euros are approved by your manager; above 1,000 euros by the finance lead. Approved claims are paid with the next payroll run, on the 28th of the month, or on the 15th for claims approved before the 10th.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "leave-and-time-off", title: "Leave and time off", body: "# Leave and time off\n\nThis page is the rule book for days away from work at Oriel. It applies to everyone on a permanent contract in France; interns and people on a fixed-term contract get the same days pro rata. If something here contradicts your contract, the contract wins, and please tell People Ops so the page gets fixed.\n\n## Paid leave\n\nEveryone earns 25 working days of paid leave a year, counted from 1 June to 31 May, which is the French reference period. Days are earned at 2.08 per month worked and can be taken as soon as they are earned. Up to five days can be carried into the next period; anything above five is lost on 31 May unless a manager has approved an exception in writing before 30 April.\n\nLeave is requested in the HR tool at least two weeks ahead for one to four days, and at least six weeks ahead for a week or more. Your manager answers within five working days. A request that gets no answer in that time is approved. Between 20 December and 2 January the office runs with a skeleton team, so leave in that window is approved by rota, first come first served, with at most two people away per team.\n\n## Public holidays\n\nThe eleven French public holidays are days off for everyone, whichever office you work from. When a holiday falls on a weekend it is not moved. The Lyon office closes for Pentecost Monday; the Paris office treats it as the solidarity day and stays open, which is why Lyon people see one fewer working day in May.\n\n## Sick leave\n\nTell your manager before 10:00 on the first day, by message or phone, and send the medical certificate to People Ops within 48 hours. Oriel tops up the social security payment to full salary from the first day for up to 90 days a year; after that the collective agreement's rates apply. Nobody is asked to work while on sick leave, and a manager who messages someone on sick leave about work will hear from People Ops.\n\n## Family leave\n\nParental leave follows the legal scheme: 16 weeks of maternity leave, 25 days of paternity and second-parent leave to be taken within six months of the birth, both paid at full salary. Oriel adds four weeks of paid leave to either, to be taken in the first year. Adoption leave gets the same addition. Carer's leave for a close relative is five paid days a year, plus unpaid leave by agreement.\n\n## Other absences\n\nMoving house is two paid days once every three years. Jury duty and the citizens' reserve are paid at full salary on presentation of the summons. Unpaid leave of up to three months can be requested after two years of service and is decided by the department head within a month.\n\n## Working from another country\n\nYou can work up to 20 days a year from another EU country without a formal request, as long as your manager knows and you keep French working hours. Anything longer, or any country outside the EU, needs a written agreement from People Ops at least a month ahead, because of tax and insurance.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "on-call-and-incidents", title: "On-call and incident response", body: "# On-call and incident response\n\nSomeone from engineering is on call every hour of the year, because a hub that will not open its doors at 06:00 stops a courier's day. This runbook is what the on-call person does; the on-call rota and the escalation phone numbers are in the paging tool.\n\n## The rotation\n\nOne primary and one secondary, a week each, Tuesday 10:00 to Tuesday 10:00. The primary gets every page; the secondary gets the page if the primary has not acknowledged within ten minutes. A week on call is paid as an allowance plus a day off in the following fortnight. Swap weeks in the paging tool, not by message, so the pages go to the right phone.\n\n## What pages\n\nThree things page: a hub offline for more than fifteen minutes, the booking API error rate above 2 % for five minutes, and a failed deployment. Everything else is a ticket for the morning. A page that was not one of these three is a bug in the alerting, and the fix is a change to the alert, made the same week.\n\n## Severity\n\nSev 1: couriers cannot badge in at more than one hub, or data is being lost. Sev 2: one hub down, or a customer-facing feature broken for everyone. Sev 3: degraded, with a workaround. Sev 1 and 2 get an incident channel and a status page entry within fifteen minutes; Sev 3 is a ticket.\n\n## The first fifteen minutes\n\nAcknowledge the page. Open the incident channel with the hub id or the service name in its title. Say what you see, not what you think caused it. Check the deployment log: the most common cause is the last deployment, and rolling it back is always allowed without asking. If a hub is offline, the second most common cause is power at the site, and the field technician on duty can go and look.\n\n## Hubs\n\nA hub keeps working without the network: the doors open for badges it has seen in the last 30 days, and the events are stored on the hub and sent when it is back. So an offline hub is urgent but not a disaster, unless couriers new to that hub are waiting. The hub's own status page is reachable on the site network from the technician's laptop. Restart order: the modem, then the controller, then the chargers one by one. Never cut the power to the whole hub with a charger in session.\n\n## Communication\n\nThe status page is updated every thirty minutes during a Sev 1 or 2, even if the update is \"still looking\". Customers with a hub down are told by their account owner, who is paged by the incident lead. Nobody speculates about causes in writing to a customer until the review is done.\n\n## After\n\nEvery Sev 1 and 2 gets a written review within five working days: the timeline, what was done, what was learned, and the actions with owners and dates. Reviews are blameless and are read at the Thursday company meeting. A Sev 3 that happens three times becomes a Sev 2 for the purpose of the review.\n\n## Handover\n\nTuesday at 10:00, fifteen minutes between the outgoing and incoming primaries: the open incidents, the hubs on watch, the deployments planned for the week. Written in the handover thread so the secondary can read it.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "onboarding-first-week", title: "Onboarding: the first week", body: "# Onboarding: the first week\n\nWelcome. The first week at Oriel is built so that by Friday you have met your team, understood what a charging hub is, shipped one small thing and know who to ask. Your manager and your buddy own this plan; People Ops checks on Wednesday that it is on track.\n\n## Before day one\n\nPeople Ops sends the contract, the IT form and the handbook link ten days before the start. IT ships the laptop to the office or to the home address for remote starters, set up and encrypted, with the password in a separate message. Your manager writes a one-page note on your first project and names a buddy from another team.\n\n## Day one, Monday\n\n09:30 at the Paris office (or the Lyon depot for field roles): badge, laptop, accounts. 10:30 the welcome session with People Ops: who is who, the handbook in twenty minutes, how pay and leave work. Lunch with the team. 14:00 with your manager: the first project, the first-week plan, how one-on-ones work. 16:00 with your buddy: the tools, the chat channels that matter, where the coffee is.\n\n## Day two, Tuesday\n\nThe product day. The morning is the hub walkthrough with a field technician: what a hub is, how a courier badges in, what the telemetry shows, what goes wrong. In the afternoon you read the three runbooks (on-call, deploying, backups) and the support SLA page, and you shadow an hour of support with the duty agent.\n\n## Day three, Wednesday\n\nYour first change. Engineers pick a starter ticket from the \"first week\" column and ship it with a reviewer by the end of the day; operations people run one full support case with their buddy; sales and account people join a customer call as an observer. People Ops checks in at 15:00 for fifteen minutes.\n\n## Day four, Thursday\n\nThe company meeting, every other Thursday at 11:00, where the week's numbers and the hub map are reviewed. On the other weeks it is the team's planning meeting. The afternoon is the security training (forty minutes, online, with a quiz you need to pass before you get access to customer data).\n\n## Day five, Friday\n\nA written note to your manager: what you understood, what surprised you, what you would change. It is read, not graded. Then the Friday demo at 16:00, where you can show the starter ticket.\n\n## The first three months\n\nWeekly one-on-ones with your manager; a thirty-day check with People Ops; a written review at the end of the trial period (two months for most roles, four for managers). The buddy stays yours for three months. The goal of the first quarter is one project you own end to end.\n\n## Who to ask\n\nAccounts and access: IT, in the portal. Pay, leave and the contract: People Ops. Anything about a hub: the duty technician, on the field channel. Anything about a customer: the account owner in the CRM. If you do not know who, ask your buddy.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "pricing-and-plans", title: "Pricing and plans", body: "# Pricing and plans\n\nOriel sells access to its charging hubs to delivery companies, not to individual couriers. The price is per badge per month, with a usage component for charging. This page is the public price list as of this year and the rules sales people apply; discounts outside these rules go through the commercial lead.\n\n## Plans\n\nStarter: up to 20 badges, 19 euros per badge per month, access to the hubs in one city, charging billed at 0.45 euros per kWh, support by email within one working day. Fleet: from 21 badges, 15 euros per badge per month, all cities, charging at 0.39 euros per kWh, a named account owner, support within four hours in working hours. Network: from 200 badges, priced per contract, with reserved slots at chosen hubs, charging at 0.35 euros per kWh, support within one hour around the clock, and quarterly reviews.\n\n## What a badge is\n\nA badge is one active courier credential. A badge is billed for every month in which it is active on at least one day. Deactivating a badge on the 3rd and reactivating it on the 25th bills one badge for that month, not two. Badges can be moved between couriers; the data of the previous courier is deleted 30 days after the move.\n\n## Charging\n\nCharging is metered per session from the slot's meter and billed monthly in arrears at the plan's rate, rounded to the cent per session. Sessions shorter than two minutes are not billed, because they are almost always a courier plugging in the wrong slot. A session interrupted by a hub fault is not billed, and the customer sees it as \"waived\" on the invoice.\n\n## Reserved slots\n\nNetwork customers can reserve slots at a hub: a reserved slot is held for the customer's badges between the hours they choose and costs 60 euros per slot per month. A reserved slot that was not used at all in a month is credited back at half price, once, as a reminder that it is probably not needed.\n\n## Discounts\n\nAnnual payment in advance: 10 % off the badge fee. A second city for a Starter customer: the plan moves to Fleet. Non-profit delivery cooperatives: 20 % off the badge fee on any plan, on presentation of the statutes. No discounts on charging, ever, because the electricity is a cost passed through at a thin margin.\n\n## Billing\n\nInvoices are issued on the first working day of the month for the previous month, due in 30 days. Payment is by direct debit on Starter, and by transfer or direct debit on the other plans. A customer 15 days late gets a reminder; at 45 days the badges are suspended with a week's notice to the account owner; at 60 days the contract is handed to finance.\n\n## Trials\n\nA new customer can run a two-week trial with up to five badges in one city, free, including charging up to 50 kWh. The trial ends with a call with the account owner. Trials are not extended; a customer who needs more time signs a monthly Starter plan instead.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "remote-work-and-equipment", title: "Remote work and equipment", body: "# Remote work and equipment\n\nOriel is an office-first company with real flexibility. Most teams are in the office three days a week, with Tuesday and Thursday as the days everyone in a team is in. The other days are yours to choose, within the rules below.\n\n## Where you can work\n\nFrom home, from either office, or from a coworking space the company has an agreement with (the list is in the IT portal). Cafés and trains are fine for reading and messages but not for calls about customers or for anything with hub telemetry on the screen. Field technicians work from the sites and from the Lyon depot; their office days are the Monday planning meeting and the monthly safety review.\n\n## Hours\n\nCore hours are 10:00 to 16:00 Paris time, when everyone is reachable. Outside core hours you set your own day within the legal limits: no more than 10 hours a day, 48 hours a week, and at least 11 hours of rest between two days. The right to disconnect is real: nobody has to read messages after 19:00 or before 08:00, and the chat tool is set to deliver scheduled messages at 08:30 by default.\n\n## Equipment\n\nEveryone gets a laptop from the catalogue (a 14-inch model by default, 16-inch on request for people who build hub firmware or run the data pipelines), a screen for home, a headset, and a phone if the role needs one. Equipment stays the company's and comes back when you leave. A broken laptop is replaced within two working days from the Paris stock; report it to IT in the portal, not by message to a colleague.\n\n## Home office allowance\n\nPeople who work from home at least two days a week get 30 euros a month, paid with the salary, for electricity and internet. It needs no receipt. A one-off budget of 300 euros for a chair and a desk is available in the first six months, on expenses with a receipt.\n\n## Security at home\n\nWork on the company laptop only, with the disk encrypted (it is by default) and the screen locked when you step away. Home Wi-Fi must have a password; a public Wi-Fi is used with the VPN on. Printing customer data at home is not allowed. Family members do not use the work laptop, even for a quick search.\n\n## Meetings\n\nA meeting with one remote person is a remote meeting: everyone joins from their own laptop with the camera on, even in the office, so nobody is half in the room. Meetings are 25 or 50 minutes long so the next one can start on time. Every recurring meeting has an owner who cancels it when there is nothing to discuss.\n\n## Leaving the company\n\nOn the last day the laptop, the badge and the phone go back to IT, and the accounts are closed the same evening. The home screen and headset can be kept for a token price of 20 euros if you have been at Oriel more than a year.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "security-and-passwords", title: "Security and passwords", body: "# Security and passwords\n\nOriel runs charging hubs on public streets and holds the badge data of thousands of couriers, so a security mistake is a safety and a privacy problem, not only an IT one. These rules are short on purpose.\n\n## Accounts\n\nOne account per person, no shared logins, ever. Every account uses the single sign-on and a hardware key or the authenticator app as the second factor; SMS is not accepted. Service accounts belong to a team, are named for what they do, and have an owner in the inventory. An account that has not been used for 90 days is disabled, and after 180 days it is deleted.\n\n## Passwords\n\nWhere a password still exists, it is at least 14 characters, generated by the password manager, and never reused. The company password manager is mandatory for every work secret; a secret in a message, a document or a spreadsheet is a security incident and is rotated the same day. Passwords are never sent by email, and nobody at Oriel will ever ask you for yours.\n\n## Devices\n\nLaptops are encrypted, lock after five minutes, and run the management agent that applies updates within seven days of release. Personal devices do not hold company data, with one exception: the chat and mail apps on a phone enrolled in the management tool. A lost or stolen device is reported to IT within one hour, at any time of day; IT wipes it remotely and the loss is logged, without blame.\n\n## Access\n\nAccess is granted to a role, not a person, and reviewed every quarter by the team lead. Production access to the hub fleet is limited to the on-call rotation and the firmware team, through the bastion, with every session recorded. Customer data is read through the support tool, never exported, and never pasted into a chat or an outside tool.\n\n## Phishing and reporting\n\nReport anything suspicious with the \"report\" button in the mail client or on the security channel. A report that turns out to be nothing is still the right call. Clicking a bad link is not a disciplinary matter; hiding that you clicked is. The security team runs a test phishing campaign each quarter and shares the results by team, never by name.\n\n## Secrets in code\n\nSecrets live in the secrets manager and reach the services as environment variables at start-up. A secret committed to a repository is rotated within the hour, even on a private branch, because history is forever. The pre-commit hook scans for keys; do not bypass it.\n\n## Incidents\n\nA security incident is anything that may have exposed data or let someone in: a lost device, a leaked secret, a phishing click with credentials entered, a strange login. Call the on-call engineer; the security lead decides within an hour whether it is a reportable breach, which under the GDPR means notifying the regulator within 72 hours. Customers are told plainly, by the account owner, with what happened and what is being done.\n" },
  { workspace: "oriel", slug: "support-slas", title: "Support SLAs", body: "# Support SLAs\n\nSupport at Oriel is the duty agent during the day and the on-call engineer at night. The promises below are in the customer contracts, so missing them is a contractual matter, not just a bad day. The support tool measures all of them automatically.\n\n## Hours\n\nWorking hours are Monday to Friday, 08:00 to 19:00 Paris time, excluding French public holidays. Network customers have support around the clock, through the on-call phone line for anything that stops couriers.\n\n## Response times\n\nThe response time is the time to the first human answer, not to the fix. Starter: one working day. Fleet: four working hours. Network: one hour, any time. An automatic acknowledgement does not count as an answer.\n\n## Resolution targets\n\nA courier blocked at a hub: two hours to a workaround on every plan, because the fallback is always to open the slot remotely or send the technician. A hub fully down: four hours to restore or to redirect the customer's badges to the nearest hub, with the charging difference waived. A billing question: five working days. A feature request: an answer within ten working days saying whether and when, never silence.\n\n## Priorities\n\nP1: couriers cannot badge in or charge. P2: one feature broken, a workaround exists. P3: a question or a small defect. P4: an idea. The customer suggests the priority and the duty agent confirms it; disagreements go to the account owner. A P1 that lasts more than four hours is also an incident for the engineering team.\n\n## Credits\n\nIf a Fleet or Network customer's hubs were unavailable for more than 0.5 % of the month (about three and a half hours), the badge fee for that month is credited by 10 %; above 2 %, by 25 %; above 5 %, by 50 %. Availability is measured per hub from the fleet service's view, excluding announced maintenance windows and site power cuts. Credits are applied on the next invoice without the customer asking.\n\n## Maintenance windows\n\nPlanned maintenance is announced at least five working days ahead on the status page and by email to the account owners, happens between 01:00 and 05:00, and never on the same hub two weeks in a row.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA customer who is not satisfied with an answer can ask for the account owner; the account owner can bring in the head of operations. Every escalation is logged with what was promised. Twice a year the account owners read the escalations together and change the rules on this page if a pattern shows.\n\n## What support does not do\n\nRepair a courier's bike, give legal advice on employment, or share another customer's data, even when the two customers share a hub.\n" },
];
